Sony NEWS HLE keyboard (news_hid): make ctlsock/browser typing byte-exact.

Two defects in the stock HLE made keyboard input through the ctlsock matrix
path unreliable on the newsos station (NEWS-OS 4.1R, nws3260), especially
inside the X (sxsession) desktop:

  1. SHIFTED CHARACTERS LAND AT THE WRONG LEVEL.  device_matrix_keyboard_
     interface scans the 8 rows round-robin, one row per 1200 Hz tick, and
     reports a changed cell in ROW order the instant it reaches that row --
     not in the order the edges were applied.  The ctlsock module (and a real
     browser typing through it) applies a Shift press and the character press
     within the same ~6.7 ms full sweep.  L Shift is on ROW3 while most
     letters/digits are on ROW0..ROW2, so the character's row is scanned first
     and the guest sees the character MAKE before the Shift MAKE, registering
     it unshifted.  Measured on the live station: `The`->`the`, `#`->`3`,
     `JUMPED`->`JUMpeD`.  Intermittent -- it depends on where the round-robin
     pointer sits when the two edges land.

     Fix: buffer a sweep's make/break edges and flush them at scan_complete()
     in a shift-correct order -- modifier makes, then character makes, then
     character breaks, then modifier breaks -- preserving per-key order within
     each class.  The sender always applies the modifier before the character
     it shifts, so stabilising the order within the sweep is sufficient.

  2. FAST BURSTS OVERFLOW THE 8-DEEP FIFO AND ARE DROPPED SILENTLY.  A shifted
     character is up to 4 scancodes; util::fifo has WriteWrap=false, so once
     full it discards new bytes.  Deepen the host FIFO to 256 so no realistic
     typed burst overruns it.

Neither is reproducible by clean, slow, one-key-at-a-time typing; both bite at
normal typing cadence where Shift and the character overlap.

